<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah [Laws of the Afternoon Prayer]<br>Halachah 7, Part 2 (§§ 17–31)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:49</span> (<i>LaShemesh Ohel Bahem</i>), continued</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 17 — Pesach and the Prohibition of <i>Chametz</i>: Haste from Incomplete <i>Is'arusa d'L'sata</i></div>

<div class="p">Pesach and the prohibition of <span class="h">chametz</span>: Israel left in haste [<span class="h">chipazon</span>] because they were not worthy of redemption through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> (which was not yet complete). They were compelled to leave in haste (= the prohibition of <span class="h">chametz</span>). Even at Sinai — though they had some preparation — they were not yet worthy of such a face-to-face prophetic revelation (the Torah was meant to be given after a thousand generations). Therefore the Adversary provoked them through the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> — the Golden Calf. One cannot enter <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> (perception of G-dliness) except according to the tikun of <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>: service, good deeds, overpowering bad thoughts — revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> through the mouth: <span class="v">"I shall make known Your <i>emunah</i> with my mouth."</span> <span class="h">Na'aseh</span> must precede <span class="h">Nishma</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The tikun through the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> (gold, silver, copper = <span class="h">guvanin ila'in</span> [supernal colors]). The essential test and free will centers on guarding the <span class="h">Bris</span> (dependent on thought). Overpowering bad thoughts → revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> → <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascends. Flawing this (wanting to perceive without purifying thought) → great corruption → the craving for money [<span class="h">ta'avas mamon</span>] overpowers (= idolatry: <span class="v">"gods of silver, gods of gold"</span>). Money at its root is exceedingly lofty — the light of abundance there is less physicalized than that drawn for procreation (which is clothed in the body's seed, where the greatest provocation lies). Sin in forbidden areas damages <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>, which then damages the <span class="h">guvanin ila'in</span> (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>), leading to idolatry or excessive craving for money (= the Golden Calf). The tikun through the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> built by Moshe through Betzalel and Ahali'av — drawing the revelation of His G-dliness and <span class="h">Malchus</span>: <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> [The essential tikun of the contraction is through speech — the speech of prayer, songs, and praises = the three daily prayers.]</div>

<div class="sn">§ 18 — The Early <i>Chasidim</i>: Silent Preparation, Then Prayer in Speech</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"The early <i>chasidim</i> would wait an hour and then pray."</span> First they would cling to the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> in wondrous d'vaikus (= the silent hour before prayer). Then, seeing they might ascend beyond measure, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, they contracted the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> in their hearts and engaged in prayer through speech — the essential tikun of the contraction: <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span> = <span class="mx">"service of the heart — this is prayer"</span> = the essential revelation of His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. Afterward they would wait another hour — elevating <span class="h">Malchus</span> (<span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>) together with <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>: <span class="v">"who is this ascending from the wilderness?"</span> — specifically through the speech of prayer. Only after <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed does <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascend.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"My heart was hot within me; in my meditation fire burned"</span> — nearly consumed by excessive enthusiasm (= the flaw of Nadav and Avihu: <span class="v">"when they drew near before Hashem"</span>). The tikun: <span class="v">"I spoke with my tongue"</span> = the speech of prayer.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 19 — Purim and the War Against Haman-Amalek: Every Good Point Matters</div>

<div class="p">Purim and the war against Haman-Amalek. The <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s entire work: to reveal His <span class="h">Malchus</span> to all — from the highest to the lowest levels — extracting even souls that fell into the deepest places. Through the above tikun: contracting the light, overpowering bad thoughts, revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span>. Even one who damaged terribly (falling into bad thoughts that are among the "twenty-four things obstructing <span class="h">teshuvah</span>") — Hashem will illuminate him wondrously, strengthening him each time against bad thoughts. He should not despair from any thoughts that pass over him — knowing that every point and movement of detaching from bad to good is exceedingly precious to Hashem.</div>

<div class="p">Haman-Amalek (encompassing all <span class="h">klipos</span>) is the opposite of all this — intent on evil from beginning to end. Drawing from the dregs of the Breaking of Vessels (= the kings of Edom who died; Haman's ten sons = the ten impure crowns). Amalek overpowers in bad thoughts: <span class="v">"every inclination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil all the day"</span> — the final letters spell <span class="h">Amalek</span>. <span class="v">"The first of the nations is Amalek, and his end is destruction"</span> — his very overpowering causes his destruction. The moment a person begins detaching thought from evil to good, Amalek confuses him — the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s war is fought for the weak of Israel. <span class="v">"Remember what Amalek did to you... he attacked the stragglers"</span> — cooling the weak.</div>

<div class="p">Only through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (Moshe, Yehoshua, master and students) can he be subdued — not all at once (Hashem desires the world's continuance, wanting all sparks clarified through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>). The essential: instilling in them the skill of <span class="mx">"expert in running forth, expert in returning"</span> — contracting enthusiasm, valuing every movement of thought from evil to good (= the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>). <span class="v">"War for Hashem against Amalek from generation to generation"</span> — through the generations born, who are taught His G-dliness (<span class="v">"from generation to generation His faithfulness"</span>). This brings Amalek's downfall from generation to generation. <span class="mx">"Purim"</span> = <span class="h">perurim</span> [crumbs]: the tikun through tiny good crumbs snatched by each person. Amalek overpowers through flawed <span class="h">Bris</span> (= thought): the <span class="h">tzadik</span> gathers the crumbs of good thoughts that Amalek tries to trample. The essential tikun through the joy of Purim (= ten types of melody = the joy of groom and bride = <span class="h">holadah</span> from generation to generation).</div>

<div class="p"><i>[Yerushalmi Shekalim Ch. 1: "Because they sinned at midday, they shall give a half-<span class="h">shekel</span>" — the half-<span class="h">shekel</span> is the tikun needed at midday = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>.]</i></div>

<div class="sn">§ 20 — Birth: The Soul's Descent Through All Worlds; the War for the Weak</div>

<div class="p">The essential <span class="h">holadah</span>: the soul must pass from above all levels — from above all four worlds (<span class="h">Atzilus</span>, <span class="h">Beriah</span>, <span class="h">Yetzirah</span>, <span class="h">Asiyah</span>), from a place where thought cannot grasp at all — descending until drawn through man's inclination and thought. It must pass through the physical body, passing twice through the place of waste (as stated in many <span class="h">sefarim</span>), born through pangs, pains, and much blood. From there it must be clarified: removing the <span class="h">orlah</span> on the eighth day, nullifying <span class="v">"foreskin of heart"</span> (= bad thoughts that block the heart) — breaking the evil thoughts of the heart day by day, until meriting what one merits according to one's holiness. The essential war is for the weak who stumbled — each time their spark awakens to return, Amalek attacks them again. They must be shown that every point of detaching thought is precious. The essential: knowing that we know nothing at all, never despairing of crying out and praying.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Mordechai"</span> (= the generation's <span class="h">tzadik</span>) <span class="v">"knew all that was done"</span> — all the trickery and cunning: Amalek's provocation through heated cravings, through <span class="h">machaloikes</span> between husband and wife, through <span class="h">machaloikes</span> in the world (especially to distance from the <span class="h">tzadik</span>). <span class="v">"He went out into the city and cried out."</span> He brought Israel in every city to fasting, weeping: <span class="v">"the fasts and their crying."</span> Through this, all was transformed to good. Through the generations born — teaching them His G-dliness from generation to generation — Amalek is brought down generation by generation.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 21 — Forbidden Investigations; Purim = Crumbs; the <i>Machatzis HaShekel</i> at Midday</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"Do not expound forbidden relations with three, nor the Act of Creation with two, nor the Chariot alone"</span> — all connected because they are interrelated. The essential test and free will centers on this craving — even within what is permitted, there are thousands of gradations; all the more so in what is forbidden. Encompassed in three aspects: the holiest (beyond which there is nothing higher), the most damaged and corrupt, and the intermediate (= <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, <span class="h">bainonim</span>, <span class="h">r'sha'im</span>). One may not expound or investigate what occurs in this realm — excessive thought even about the negative aspect is harmful. All the generations of birth are also three: father, mother, and the child born — how the soul is drawn through this is exceedingly hidden. <span class="h">Ma'aseh Bereishis</span> = two aspects: before and after Creation. <span class="h">Ma'aseh Merkavah</span> = how potential and actual are encompassed in one (= the <span class="h">ko'ach hahiyuli</span> [primordial force]) = unity. All are forbidden to expound inappropriately — because the cravings rooted there derive from the dregs of Amalek.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You made him a little less than G-d"</span> (= the contraction); <span class="v">"You crowned him with <i>kavod</i> and glory"</span> (= all the <span class="h">kavod</span> from this). <span class="h">Purim</span> = <span class="h">perurim</span> [crumbs]: <span class="v">"it crumbled — the earth crumbled."</span> The essential tikun through tiny crumbs — each person snatching what he can. <i>[Yerushalmi Shekalim: "Because they sinned at midday, they shall give a half-<span class="h">shekel</span>" — the half-<span class="h">shekel</span> is the tikun needed at midday = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>.]</i></div>

<div class="sn">§ 22 — The Contractions of the <i>Chalal HaPanui</i> Apply to Every Person: <i>Ratzo V'Shov</i></div>

<div class="p">To explain further: Rabbainu hints in this teaching that the entire matter of the contraction of the <span class="h">Chalal HaPanui</span> (described in the Zohar and the beginning of the Eitz Chaim) — all applies to every Jew, who is a portion of G-d from above: <span class="v">"in the image of G-d He made man."</span> All the contractions of Creation's beginning were solely for man. He contracted Himself in wondrous contractions, revealing His G-dliness through wisdom — all for creating the worlds (= qualities), all so man would be created in <span class="h">Olam HaAsiyah</span> with free will, through which His <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed.</div>

<div class="p">The Ari"zal describes the wondrous contractions that preceded (the <span class="h">Olam HaMalbush</span> — not described in the Eitz Chaim but in other writings): many aspects of contractions in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> [running forth and returning] (= <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>) before everything. All is encompassed in what Rabbainu writes (<span class="lr">LM I:30</span>, "<span class="h">Mishrah</span>"): "Perceptions of G-dliness can only be attained through many contractions from cause to effect, from higher intellect to lower... a great master is needed." The principle: at first, the light was contracted; then some light was drawn, which then returned — leaving only a <span class="h">reshimu</span> (the light could not yet be endured). Then the light was drawn again in a new wondrous contraction, then concealed again — and so many times (= the <span class="h">Tehiru Ila'ah</span>, the <span class="h">Olam HaMalbush</span> which folded the light, until the <span class="h">Chalal HaPanui</span> was formed). Then the light of the <span class="h">Melachim</span> [kings] was drawn — they could not endure and broke and died — until the World of Tikun began. All for man — the breaking/death was because there was not yet <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>.</div>

<div class="p">When the man of free will came (created in the image of G-d) — upon him depends the tikun of all worlds. All the above contractions occur within man: the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span>'s enthusiasm of his heart, which must be contracted. Every contraction Hashem made to reveal His G-dliness — all must pass through man (for all was only for man's sake, to tikun through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>). All illumination and concealment — <span class="v">"indeed You are a G-d who conceals"</span>; <span class="v">"a moment in His wrath, life in His will... Hashem, in Your will You established my mountain — You hid Your face and I was terrified"</span>; <span class="v">"the voice of my beloved knocks"</span> (= revelation), <span class="v">"my beloved slipped away"</span> (= concealment) — all is for free will. Therefore whoever walks in Hashem's ways must be <span class="mx">"expert in running forth, expert in returning"</span>. All the contractions are <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>. All angels and <span class="h">seraphim</span> perceive the Creator through <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>: <span class="v">"the living beings ran forth and returned."</span> But the essential is man — who through free will can tikun the balance, serving in measure and gradation. The great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> tikun the contraction so that His G-dliness is revealed to all in measure. The essential: through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> by man below.</div>

<div class="p">This occurs every day — <span class="mx">"in His goodness He renews the act of Creation daily"</span>: <span class="v">"He rolls light before darkness and darkness before light"</span> — all for Israel's benefit. We say this before <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> (= the revelation of His unity and <span class="h">Malchus</span>). The changes (light and darkness) derive from the primordial contractions. All depends on man now: <span class="v">"the earth He gave to mankind"</span> — the power to draw His G-dliness through the above contractions. Each time the contraction stirs (from which the evil inclination draws power through bad thoughts) — one must overpower with good thoughts, serve in measure, reveal His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. Then <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascends — and the next day, at a higher level. This is the daily renewal of Creation through man.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 23 — Ascents and Descents: Every Arousal Matters; the <i>Tzadikim</i> Gather Good Points</div>

<div class="p">All this occurs for the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> at every level — and especially for those who must do <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (sin = destruction of worlds and the above tikun). The world endures through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who draw His mercy each time, drawing tikun in wondrous ways — illuminating each person's heart, awakening them to Hashem, giving deep counsel and wondrous strengthening so they can return from wherever they are. Yet some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> is essential — without it, nothing is possible.</div>

<div class="p">This is all the thousands and myriads of ascents and descents every person experiences — even those very far from Hashem have ascents and descents (no day is like another). Especially those awakening to return and drawing near to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — weak of strength, the evil inclination attacks them more fiercely. They experience ascents and descents without measure: <span class="v">"they ascend to heaven, descend to depths."</span> Tremendous strengthening is needed — especially in these generations near <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, when the Adversary intensifies more. <span class="v">"Upon whom can we rely? Only upon our Father in Heaven."</span> The essential: the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who stood in this world's war in ultimate perfection. They teach us: no arousal toward Hashem is ever lost, even from the lowest depths. Through this, wondrous tikun is made. Every good point — even from the worst of the worst — is exceedingly precious to them. They gather all good points, every arousal, and build wondrous tikun from them. The essential: do not mock or scoff at those who work on your tikun. Strengthen yourself to fulfill their words — awaken anew at every moment, as long as the soul is within you. <span class="v">"My eyes are always toward Hashem"</span> — until He looks down and sees from Heaven. Then you will merit a good and wondrous end — a great share in <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> through which all worlds are repaired.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 24 — <i>Minchah</i> = Between Day and Day: <i>Bain HaShmashos</i>; Descent for the Sake of Ascent</div>

<div class="p">All this is <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> — between one day and the next: the departure of this day and the entry of tomorrow. This contains a wondrous and awesome secret — the beginning of the contraction (= <span class="h">bain hashmashos</span>, the blink of an eye: one enters and one departs — impossible to grasp). The tikun is through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> (= <span class="h">tefilah</span>) — therefore <span class="h">Minchah</span> was established then, with great care. One must pray with all one's heart — repairing the contraction between days: the departure of this day's light of knowledge and the entry of a new, contracted light for tomorrow. No two days are alike — each day Hashem renews Creation with new intellect. Though it seems one forgets Him more as days pass, the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of each generation recognize Him anew each day, drawing His G-dliness through wondrous new contractions.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Bain ha'arbayim</span> [between the evenings] = between levels. For the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>: descent = some withdrawal of holy <span class="h">da'as</span> (= twilight = dimming of intellect's light). For most of the world: actual descent through sins. The <span class="h">tzadikim</span> labor to bring them to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: at <span class="h">bain ha'arbayim</span> (= <span class="h">Minchah</span>). Sin = withdrawal of light. But since there was already some light (even sinners are full of <span class="h">mitzvos</span> like a pomegranate) — after the light was concealed through sins, the essential work is returning with increased light: <span class="v">"return us, Hashem, to You, and we shall return; renew our days as of old [<i>k'kedem</i>]"</span> — as at the beginning of Creation: the light shone and was concealed, and so many times (= all for tikun = free will). May He return us in <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, renewing us from day to day — that through the very descent we ascend higher: <span class="mx">"descent for the purpose of ascent."</span> Since light existed and was concealed, it can afterward be drawn with even greater abundance and wondrous renewal — as at Creation's beginning. All through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — but we must awaken to approach them (without <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, nothing is possible).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 25 — "Let My Prayer Be Prepared as Incense... the Offering of Evening"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Let my prayer be prepared as incense before You — the lifting of my hands, an evening offering [<i>minchas erev</i>]."</span> The essential prayer is <span class="h">minchas erev</span> (= <span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span>) = <span class="h">hisbodedus</span> and personal conversation [<span class="h">sichah</span>] with the Creator: <span class="v">"he pours out his conversation before Hashem."</span> When one sees the light departing (= <span class="h">bain ha'arbayim</span>), one must strengthen in prayer: <span class="v">"Yitzchak went out to converse [<i>lasu'ach</i>] in the field."</span> Pour out the heart like water before Hashem — before the day passes: <span class="v">"woe to us, for the day has turned, the evening shadows stretch."</span> How much more so when most of one's days have passed — the day begins to darken: <span class="v">"remember your Creator... before the sun and light grow dark."</span> All this = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 26 — <i>Parshas HaChodesh</i>: Sanctifying the New Moon = Repairing the Contraction</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Parshas HaChodesh</span>: <span class="h">Kiddush HaChodesh</span> (sanctifying the new moon) = repairing the moon's blemish from the above. The two luminaries' light is Hashem's light (= the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span>). Originally the light was immensely great (our Sages: one could see from one end of the world to the other). Hashem stored it — seeing the wicked would misuse it. Excessive light = destruction. Only the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> can use it (knowing to contract even immense light). Each person has his own boundary: <span class="v">"you, your boundary... the people shall not break through."</span> The two luminaries were then created. The moon complained: <span class="mx">"two kings cannot share one crown"</span> — wanting the greater, stored light. Hashem said: <span class="v">"go and diminish yourself"</span> — whoever wants to gaze beyond measure must diminish himself further (= contraction: <span class="mx">"whoever is great, let him become small"</span>).</div>

<div class="p">The moon argued further (our Sages: R' Shimon ben Pazi's teaching [Chulin]). Hashem said: <span class="h">tzadikim</span> shall be called by your name — <span class="mx">"Yaakov the small, David the small, Shmuel the small"</span> — the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who make themselves small. David said: <span class="v">"Hashem, my heart is not haughty... I did not walk in great and wondrous things beyond me — I calmed and quieted my soul like a weaned child."</span> Through this: light drawn in measure and gradation → the great light in tikun (= <span class="v">"who is this ascending?"</span>) = tikun and filling of the moon's blemish. <span class="h">Kiddush HaChodesh</span> (Moshe's difficulty; the secret of the intercalation given only to the great) = the secret of the contraction: <span class="h">bain hashmashos</span>, impossible to grasp. Therefore <span class="h">Parshas HaChodesh</span> was revealed to Moshe at <span class="h">bain hashmashos</span> (Rashi: <span class="v">"near sunset... He showed it to him as it grew dark"</span>). <span class="h">Kiddush HaChodesh</span> = <span class="h">bain hashmashos</span> = tikun of the contraction = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> (after midday, between the evenings, through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 27 — <i>Chametz</i> and <i>Matzah</i>: Fermenting the Mind in Holiness; Korban Pesach After Midday</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Parshas HaChodesh</span> includes the Pesach offering, eating <span class="h">matzah</span>, and the prohibition of <span class="h">chametz</span> — all connected to the above. <span class="h">Chametz</span> = <span class="h">chimutz hamoach</span> [fermenting of the brain]. The tikun: contracting the light's enthusiasm properly, overpowering bad thoughts — fermenting the brain only with holy thoughts (how to serve Hashem, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>). Then <span class="h">chametz</span> draws from holiness. But at Pesach, having received the light without proper gradation, <span class="h">chametz</span> is forbidden — only <span class="h">matzah</span> (= haste), lest the brain's fermentation from the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> overpower. The <span class="h">Korban Pesach</span> and <span class="h">chametz</span> prohibition begins after midday (= <span class="h">Minchah</span> time) — repairing the contraction between days. On <span class="h">Erev Pesach</span>, the light of Pesach (= renewal of the world, since the world's sustenance depended on Torah, whose beginning was the Exodus) must be drawn through contraction with some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — through the <span class="h">Korban Pesach</span> and <span class="h">chametz</span> prohibition beginning at midday (= <span class="h">Minchah</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 28 — The <i>Erev Rav</i>'s Damage at <i>Minchah</i> Time: <i>"Ba Sheish"</i></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Erev Rav</span>'s flaw in the Calf was at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time (after midday): <span class="v">"the people saw — <i>ki ba sheish</i>"</span> (the sixth hour arrived). They did not contract the light properly, did not sanctify their thought — wanting to gaze at what was not fitting for them. Their thoughts and deeds were impure; their minds were inflated — understanding Moshe's greatness through the knowledge that shone in them and the signs and wonders. They followed him — but never contracted the light, never stood in the battle of thought. Therefore Hashem did not want to accept them; Moshe thought they would convert sincerely. They corrupted through all the above flaws (excessive light + flawed thought) → the Calf. The essential corruption at the sixth hour — at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time — when one must repair the contraction.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 29 — <i>Rosh HaShanah</i> and the Sin of Adam</div>

<div class="p">The essential tikun on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> (= <span class="h">Pachad Yitzchak</span> = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>; Yitzchak established <span class="h">Minchah</span>). On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> the world was created — the contraction of Creation's beginning. Without <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, tikun required Adam HaRishon's prayer. But he sinned with the Tree of Knowledge (gazing where he had no permission, sinning in thought [Zohar: <span class="mx">"in thought he sinned"</span>]). Hashem had mercy and worked on his tikun — all after midday (<span class="h">Minchah</span> time): <span class="mx">"in the tenth [hour] they sinned."</span> <span class="v">"They heard Hashem's voice walking in the garden toward the wind of the day."</span> All his corruption and tikun related to the contraction — therefore at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time. The essential tikun on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>: Israel precede with <span class="h">teshuvah</span> in Elul — the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> especially. Through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, the tikun of Creation's contraction is drawn.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 30 — The Four <i>Parshiyos</i> Before Pesach</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Parshas Shekalim</span> (§10): tikun of the balance — receiving light in gradation = building the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>, through which <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascends) = <span class="h">Minchah</span>. The <span class="h">shekalim</span> atoned for the Calf (which they sinned after the sixth hour = <span class="h">Minchah</span> time). The Yerushalmi: <span class="mx">"because they sinned at midday — they shall give a half-<i>shekel</i>."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Parshas Zachor</span>: obliterating Amalek's memory = the battle of thought (overpowering bad thoughts after the contraction). Amalek attacks through bad thoughts (<span class="v">"he chanced upon you on the way"</span> = <span class="h">mikreh</span> = nocturnal occurrence from bad thoughts), external wisdoms, confusion leading to heresy. One must fight with the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s power (Moshe, Yehoshua, the elders). <span class="v">"Remember well all that Amalek did"</span> — to guard one's thought. <span class="v">"A hand upon the throne of <i>Y-H</i> — war for Hashem against Amalek from generation to generation"</span>: the <span class="h">shem</span> [Name] and <span class="h">kisei</span> [throne] are incomplete until Amalek is obliterated. <span class="h">Shem</span> = <span class="h">Malchus</span> (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>); <span class="h">kisei</span> = the throne of judgment (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>). Hashem Himself fights Amalek — <span class="v">"I shall not enter the city above"</span> until His Name is magnified to the most distant. <span class="h">Kimu v'kiblu</span> [they established and accepted — Purim] = <span class="h">na'aseh v'nishma</span>: <span class="h">kimu</span> (= <span class="h">na'aseh</span> — establishing the Torah in deed) before <span class="h">kiblu</span> (= <span class="h">nishma</span> — receiving the light in measure).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 31 — <i>Parshas Parah</i>: Purifying from Death's Impurity; Sweetening the Contraction at Its Root</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Parshas Parah Adumah</span>: purifying from the impurity of death. Death came from excessive light (= the death of the <span class="h">Melachim</span> [kings] in the Ari"zal). Adam HaRishon should have repaired this — his sin brought the decree of death. The tikun through the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> and its offerings (atoning for sins from flawed contraction). The <span class="h">Parah Adumah</span> is an even greater <span class="h">chiddush</span>: its procedure is outside — it is <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (atoning for the Calf: <span class="v">"the face of an ox from the left"</span> = flawed contractions). All sins come from failure to properly tikun the contraction from beginning to end. The <span class="h">Parah</span> (= <span class="h">gevurah</span>, left side, deep red = intense judgment/contraction) — through the <span class="h">Kohain</span>'s great power (essentially Moshe's — all <span class="h">Paros</span> are called by his name) — is taken outside the camp and slaughtered facing the entrance of the <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span> (= the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> = <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> and <span class="h">ila'ah</span>). Through this, all contractions are repaired from beginning to end — sweetening judgments at their root — nullifying the side of death. The extreme stringencies in purifying the <span class="h">Parah</span> (bringing pure children on oxen, etc., as in Maseches <span class="h">Parah</span>) reflect the need to draw the contractions from beginning to end in wondrous purity. <span class="v">"Cedar wood, hyssop, and scarlet thread"</span> — Rashi: whoever was tall as a cedar should lower himself like a hyssop and scarlet worm = <span class="mx">"whoever is great, let him be small."</span> The tikun through self-diminishment — the essential of Moshe Rabbainu's humility.</div>

<div class="p">Then <span class="h">Parshas HaChodesh</span>: <span class="h">Kiddush HaChodesh</span> = tikun and filling of the moon's blemish = repairing the contractions. Thus, before Pesach we read four <span class="h">parshiyos</span> — all to draw the holiness of Pesach and Shavuos (Exodus and <span class="h">Matan Torah</span>) = revealing His G-dliness in measure, in tikun, so His <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed to all forever — through the tikun of the contractions via <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>.</div>

